WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:02.465
- A legacy in Silicon Valley.

00:00:02.465 --> 00:00:05.070
- Palo Alto's kind of this mythical place.

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:08.430
- Humans have always searched
for mythical, magical places.

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:11.520
Atlantis, El Dorado, Shangri-La,

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.033
but none of them have actually
been found besides one.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:19.920
It started on a typical workday for me

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.750
when I was sifting through maps

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.813
which, yes, is something
I routinely just do.

00:00:24.813 --> 00:00:25.646
Oh my God.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.130
Paper maps, digitized maps, animated maps.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:33.330
I just can't get enough.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:35.700
So I'm sitting there
looking through some maps

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.053
and I stumble upon this.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.190
This is a super old British
map of North America

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:44.610
with a giant island off the West Coast.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:47.013
The island is labeled California.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.330
Okay, so clearly some cartographer

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:54.540
just got this totally wrong
and made a mistake, right?

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:58.538
But wait, here's another
and another and another.

00:00:58.538 --> 00:01:02.121
(mid tempo rhythmic music)

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.440
What is going on here?

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:08.370
This one's from 1785 or from 1750.

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:12.450
This one's from 1690 and from 1650.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.550
Like, these maps of
California as an island

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.550
are spanning over 200 years.

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:19.680
Drawn by the Dutch and the Germans

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.450
and the British and the Japanese,

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:24.873
everyone is making a map
showing California as an island.

00:01:27.943 --> 00:01:29.520
Is this like a tectonic plates thing?

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:30.990
Like, was California an island?

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:34.353
Because as we dug, we found
thousands of these maps.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.373
California as an island everywhere.

00:01:41.892 --> 00:01:43.740
I had to get to the bottom of this

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:46.710
to figure out what was going on here.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:48.480
Luckily, I live in Washington DC

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.675
so there's somewhere I need to
go visit to figure this out.

00:01:51.675 --> 00:01:54.925
(uptempo string music)

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.163
- [Train Announcer] This is
Metro Center transfer point.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:14.592
- Hey, in case anyone is in DC

00:02:14.592 --> 00:02:18.780
and is looking for a nuclear
fallout shelter, I found one.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:20.193
The Library of Congress.

00:02:33.874 --> 00:02:37.457
(suspenseful string music)

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:44.373
- [Library Worker] Hi there.

00:02:44.373 --> 00:02:46.890
- [Johnny] That's also my
background as well, that map.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:48.366
- Really?
- Yeah, yes.

00:02:48.366 --> 00:02:50.343
I love it, it's just so beautiful.

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:56.523
Look at this puppy.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.133
Wow.

00:03:03.117 --> 00:03:03.950
Wow.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:07.500
Oh my God.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:09.540
So wait, this is really weird.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:13.380
I'm looking at these original maps, 1600s.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.453
California is an island.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:20.793
But then look at this one.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.284
This is a map from like
a few decades earlier

00:03:26.284 --> 00:03:30.750
and it shows California
accurately mapped earlier,

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:33.870
like, decades earlier in like the 1500s.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:34.950
They had it right

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.750
and it looks pretty
accurate to the California

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:37.770
we know today.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:39.390
And then, suddenly,

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:41.190
the whole world started mapping California

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:42.023
as an island.

00:03:43.228 --> 00:03:44.061
What?

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:46.465
This is super weird.

00:03:46.465 --> 00:03:49.882
(mid tempo techno music)

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:52.823
I vow now

00:03:52.823 --> 00:03:55.151
to figure out what the hell
is going on with California.

00:03:55.151 --> 00:03:57.984
(uplifting music)

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:04.860
This first started as a nerdy
history cartography question

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:07.620
but soon we fell into a way deeper story

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:09.510
than we signed up for.

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:13.140
The story of a mythical
place called California,

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:16.530
full of gold and magic and dreams,

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:20.550
fueled by people who weren't
totally grounded in reality

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:23.460
but rather in dreams of something bigger,

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:24.990
something different,

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:28.113
something that may or may not truly exist.

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:31.170
This island teaches us something bigger

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:33.120
than just how humans draw maps,

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.450
it's actually more about how
humans want to see the world,

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:39.423
about how fake ideas spread and why.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:43.590
So let me show you how,
for hundreds of years,

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:47.763
California was an island
and how it still kind of is.

00:04:48.672 --> 00:04:51.255
(upbeat music)

