00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:34.750
So now I'm headed back to the studio

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:38.640
to figure out what the hell
is going on with California

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:39.993
being mapped as an island.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:44.703
One, two, three, four, five.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:48.690
Okay, this is California

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:51.690
and this is Baja, California,
which is a part of Mexico.

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:53.760
And this is the Sea of Cortez.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:55.410
It's not a full-blown sea,

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:57.477
it's actually a gulf,
the Gulf of California.

00:06:57.477 --> 00:06:59.280
But we call it the Sea of Cortez

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:00.420
and you're about to find out why.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:01.560
I actually went here once

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:02.978
when I was a 19 year-old Mormon missionary

00:07:02.978 --> 00:07:06.000
living in Baja, California for two years.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:07.560
Anyway, that is not important.

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:10.803
Let's talk about how
this became an island.

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:15.558
California originally was an idea, a myth.

00:07:15.558 --> 00:07:19.200
It was a fictional setting
written into this novel

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:21.150
from 1510 in Spain,

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:22.770
like, 500 years ago.

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:26.160
California was like Atlantis
or the Garden of Eden.

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:27.090
The author described it

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:29.670
as an island populated by black women,

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:32.100
without any men existing there.

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:33.175
On the entire island

00:07:33.175 --> 00:07:35.730
there was no metal other than gold

00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:39.780
and it was ruled by Queen
Calafia, hence California.

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:41.070
Again, it was a fake place,

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:41.903
this is not a real place.

00:07:41.903 --> 00:07:44.550
This is a guy writing a
novel about a fake place.

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:47.370
But while the author doesn't
say exactly where this is,

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:49.237
he basically says that it is, quote,

00:07:49.237 --> 00:07:51.960
"at the right hand of the Indies."

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:54.360
Reminder that the Indies is like over here

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:55.530
in Southeast Asia.

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:57.420
This novel was published in Spain

00:07:57.420 --> 00:08:00.060
about 50 years after the
invention of the printing press.

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:03.270
So printed books were like a
new technology, a big thing,

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:04.710
and this book was really popular.

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:05.543
Oh, and by the way,

00:08:05.543 --> 00:08:07.797
the book also mentions that
the women in California

00:08:07.797 --> 00:08:09.300
these like women warriors,

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:13.110
were, quote, "of beautiful
and robust bodies."

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:15.000
So you have new technology, books,

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:16.590
and beautifully robust women,

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:19.830
basically clickbait for
Spanish sailors in the 1500s.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:21.300
They were out there exploring the world

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:24.210
and reading this book
while they were out to sea.

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:25.421
Oh, and remember, at this time,

00:08:25.421 --> 00:08:28.320
these Spanish sailors are
out exploring the world

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:30.060
that Columbus had run into

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:32.860
when he was trying to find a
quicker way to get to Asia.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:36.532
So it's like 10 years after Columbus

00:08:36.532 --> 00:08:39.750
and Spain is sending people
to explore the new world

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:42.750
and by explore we mean wink
wink, extract resources

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:45.540
and like destroy the
population and make Spain rich.

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:48.431
And one of those expeditions
had this guy on board,

00:08:48.431 --> 00:08:49.980
Hernán Cortés.

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:51.090
He came over as a young dude

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:55.410
but he quickly became one of
the most shrewd, aggressive,

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:58.470
like detail-oriented
colonizers that Spain had.

00:08:58.470 --> 00:08:59.610
He goes on a big expedition

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:01.697
where he takes his 500 men and 16 horses

00:09:01.697 --> 00:09:03.270
to go look for gold.

00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:06.210
But Cortés was secretly
harboring another goal

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:07.590
for this expedition,

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:09.030
he was reading the romance novel

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:10.044
that everyone else was reading

00:09:10.044 --> 00:09:13.371
and he kind of wanted to find California,

00:09:13.371 --> 00:09:15.450
the fictional place in the book.

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:17.962
This would be like some
guy being out in like Nepal

00:09:17.962 --> 00:09:19.200
on a hike or something

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:21.540
and he's like, I secretly
wanna find Middle Earth.

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:24.280
This kind of reminds me of
when I was in Wales in 2011

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:29.460
and I like secretly
wanted to find Hogwarts.

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:31.320
Like that kind of actually happened,

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:32.153
I was young.

00:09:32.153 --> 00:09:34.050
And I was like, what if
I stumble upon Hogwarts?

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:35.880
Like, I get it Cortés, I get it.

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:36.713
You read it in the book

00:09:36.713 --> 00:09:38.340
and you're like this
place is probably real.

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:40.560
Cortés, a legitimate Spanish Explorer,

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:43.053
is now looking for a fake place in a book.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:46.980
So Cortés, after he stumbles
upon a great civilization,

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:48.840
allies with their
enemies and destroys them

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:51.090
with his guns and his smallpox,

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:53.910
small side note that can be
covered in a different time.

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:55.320
He eventually makes it over here.

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:56.340
Oh, by the way,

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:58.470
Cortés was kind of going
rogue this whole time,

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:00.390
he didn't have permission to do this.

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:02.937
And the Spanish leadership
were kind of freaking out.

00:10:02.937 --> 00:10:05.700
So much so that Cortés had
to write a bunch of letters

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:08.400
justifying how good all
of this conquest was.

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:10.110
Anyway, Cortés sends out a mission

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:12.360
to go explore a little
further off this coast,

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:13.320
like around here.

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:15.063
Holy shit, they found something!

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:18.630
Cortés is so giddy
about this new discovery

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:20.160
that he writes a letter back to the king

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:22.210
saying that his men
talked to some natives.

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:24.000
(speaking in foreign language)

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:26.123
who, quote, "affirm
that there is an island

00:10:26.123 --> 00:10:28.890
(speaking in foreign language)

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:30.270
inhabited only by women.

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:31.620
And there are no men,

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:33.000
that men come every once in a while

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:33.893
for them to like conceive

00:10:33.893 --> 00:10:35.948
but they only keep the female children.

00:10:35.948 --> 00:10:38.100
(speaking in foreign language)

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:39.343
It's only 10 days away

00:10:39.343 --> 00:10:40.890
(speaking in foreign language)

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:44.850
and that this island is
very rich in pearls, perlas,

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:46.470
and gold, y oro."

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:47.700
What?

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:48.783
Holy shit.

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:52.396
No men, only women, rich in gold.

00:10:52.396 --> 00:10:55.229
(mid tempo music)

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:02.280
And where does Cortés
think he is right now?

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:04.620
Well, back when the world looked like this

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:06.000
in these people's minds,

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:09.843
Cortés is just to the right of the Indies,

00:11:11.199 --> 00:11:15.789
which is exactly the location
described in the book

00:11:15.789 --> 00:11:17.463
for California.

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:22.203
Cortés just discovered California.

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:26.490
Oh man, this is amazing.

00:11:26.490 --> 00:11:28.459
I can feel the excitement of Cortés

00:11:28.459 --> 00:11:31.800
who literally has spent years
thinking about this island

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:34.800
and now he thinks he's
found it, California.

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:37.530
But this is not what the
king of Spain signed up for.

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:39.225
So the king of Spain's like, no, dude, no,

00:11:39.225 --> 00:11:41.700
we're not looking for
like mythical islands.

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:43.350
So he cuts off all of the funding

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:45.239
for any of these like expeditions.

00:11:45.239 --> 00:11:49.110
So Cortés turns to his own personal wealth

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:52.170
and starts funding these
expeditions on his own,

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:54.960
to go map out and find
California, the island.

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:57.330
- Now I have to go to California.

00:11:57.330 --> 00:11:58.794
- He personally goes on a voyage

00:11:58.794 --> 00:12:01.290
and starts mapping and mapping,

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:02.340
and, lo and behold,

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:04.530
he's finding what looks like an island.

00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:07.228
And they set up a colony
right here on this island

00:12:07.228 --> 00:12:09.927
and they name the island California,

00:12:09.927 --> 00:12:13.027
after the island of Queen
Calafia from the book.

00:12:13.027 --> 00:12:16.352
(joyful music)

00:12:16.352 --> 00:12:17.220
Okay, okay, okay.

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:18.420
Spoiler alert.

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:21.120
Remember that Cortés is like right here.

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:23.250
He's looking up this massive gulf

00:12:23.250 --> 00:12:25.380
that he thinks is like a full-blown sea

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:26.910
that you can like circumnavigate,

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:29.130
but he's just like at the
bottom of Baja, California,

00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:30.360
the peninsula.

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:32.550
And, worst of all, he's
burning his own cash

00:12:32.550 --> 00:12:35.340
to keep exploring this
California island idea.

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:36.690
He's got California fever.

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:37.710
Maybe the first

00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:39.990
but definitely not the last to have it.

00:12:39.990 --> 00:12:41.370
- We're going to California.

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:44.310
- So Cortés keeps going and
he's feeling really excited.

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:46.080
He's like, if this is an island

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:48.204
then that means that this
sea probably extends up

00:12:48.204 --> 00:12:50.700
and over the top of North America.

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:52.230
And maybe there's a whole strait

00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:53.880
that goes all the way back to Spain,

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:56.847
making it super easy for us
to get between Spain and Asia.

00:12:56.847 --> 00:12:59.770
And so he keeps mapping
and mapping and mapping and

00:13:02.250 --> 00:13:04.757
oh no, womp womp,

00:13:04.757 --> 00:13:07.260
Cortés ruined the myth for everyone,

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:10.200
and he like wasted away a bunch
of his money in the process.

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:11.033
But wait a minute,

00:13:11.033 --> 00:13:13.680
this is like still the
beginning of the story.

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:15.750
Like, you see how much
longer is left in this video.

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:17.520
Like, this isn't the story.

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:19.860
What's crazy is what happens next.

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:21.780
Because even though Cortés found out

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:23.160
that California wasn't an island,

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:25.653
this was all before this happened.

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:30.324
And this is where the
story gets very juicy.

00:13:30.324 --> 00:13:31.860
I am H-O.

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:33.990
Because somehow, even after all of this,

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:37.500
California still somehow managed
to make the world believe

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:40.533
that it was an island
for hundreds of years.

00:13:43.230 --> 00:13:44.160
Okay, so after the whole

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:46.410
Cortés California dreaming incident,

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:48.600
Spain went on to ignore mapping California

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:49.530
for a very long time.

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:51.150
I mean, they were really busy conquering

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:52.410
and destroying and pillaging

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:54.900
a lot of other places in the region.

00:13:54.900 --> 00:13:56.640
That is until the late 1500s,

00:13:56.640 --> 00:13:58.590
where they catch wind of a British sailor

00:13:58.590 --> 00:14:00.900
who happens to be their
literal sworn enemy,

00:14:00.900 --> 00:14:02.201
who apparently just landed here

00:14:02.201 --> 00:14:04.020
in what was gonna become Oregon

00:14:04.020 --> 00:14:05.760
to claim all of this land for Britain.

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:07.530
And Spain was like, oh shit,

00:14:07.530 --> 00:14:09.720
we gotta get back in the
game up there in that place,

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:10.553
what was it called again?

00:14:10.553 --> 00:14:11.824
Cortés called it like California

00:14:11.824 --> 00:14:14.100
after like from the novel or something.

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:15.895
Anyway, let's get some guys back up there

00:14:15.895 --> 00:14:17.172
to like map it out.

00:14:17.172 --> 00:14:18.600
We can't be asleep at the wheel

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:19.800
while the British are showing up

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:21.510
on the West Coast of North America.

00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:23.190
So they send out this expedition of guys

00:14:23.190 --> 00:14:25.530
to go map the West Coast of California.

00:14:25.530 --> 00:14:27.525
Because, after all, if
you can map something

00:14:27.525 --> 00:14:28.650
you can conquer it.

00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:30.600
That's all they were thinking
about here was Britain.

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:32.460
Okay, and this is where things get so good

00:14:32.460 --> 00:14:34.980
all because of another California dreamer.

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:39.030
This guy, Fray Antonio de la Ascensión,

00:14:39.030 --> 00:14:42.210
a Spanish dude who wasn't
really a sailor or an explorer

00:14:42.210 --> 00:14:43.410
or a cartographer,

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:45.360
he was kind of like a Catholic priest.

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:48.600
Aboard this expedition
there were legit map makers

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:51.120
and Fray Antonio was
literally the assistant.

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:52.800
He had some knowledge of map-making

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:54.690
but like he was literally the plan B

00:14:54.690 --> 00:14:56.760
in case the main map maker died.

00:14:56.760 --> 00:14:59.640
His job was really just to be religious.

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:01.440
Boy, did he do a lot more than that.

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:03.330
Fray Antonio, the peon assistant,

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:05.160
is actually the most important person

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:06.330
in this entire story,

00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:08.190
because he had an agenda.

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:11.340
He really wanted Spain to
settle more aggressively

00:15:11.340 --> 00:15:12.173
in California.

00:15:12.173 --> 00:15:13.680
He felt the pull of California,

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:15.870
this mythical cool place on the West Coast

00:15:15.870 --> 00:15:18.180
and it was his life's
mission to sell the idea

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:19.530
of Spain coming to California

00:15:19.530 --> 00:15:21.300
and setting up more permanent settlements.

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:24.570
No one on board this expedition
was on the same page,

00:15:24.570 --> 00:15:27.120
they were there just simply
mapping the West Coast,

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:27.953
that's all they were doing.

00:15:27.953 --> 00:15:29.160
So they're going along this route,

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:31.500
Fray Antonio is on the
ship probably really bored,

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:33.240
'cause he's literally not doing anything.

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:35.160
He's looking overboard at this land

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:36.009
sort of in the distance,

00:15:36.009 --> 00:15:39.330
and he starts to see
everything through his lens

00:15:39.330 --> 00:15:40.890
of total bias.

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:42.840
This bias that California was amazing

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:43.950
and that Spain should settle it.

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:44.997
So they're passing like right here

00:15:44.997 --> 00:15:46.620
on their way to the West Coast

00:15:46.620 --> 00:15:48.420
and Fray Antonio writes,

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:51.930
quote, "Here begins the entrance
of the sea of California

00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:55.468
which continues all the way
up north into this strait

00:15:55.468 --> 00:15:58.050
that actually goes above North America.

00:15:58.050 --> 00:15:59.970
And that, if you go, you can find passage

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:02.067
and navigation to Spain."

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:04.080
Wait, what?

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:07.041
Antonio, you're sitting on
the side of a boat right here

00:16:07.041 --> 00:16:09.360
looking at basically this.

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:12.180
And from that you've deduced
that California is an island

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:13.882
and that there's a passage
above North America

00:16:13.882 --> 00:16:15.690
that goes back to Spain?

00:16:15.690 --> 00:16:17.940
I swear California does this to people.

00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:21.210
It makes them ignore reality
in the name of a big dream.

00:16:21.210 --> 00:16:23.550
- Yet the thought of
giving up everything here

00:16:23.550 --> 00:16:26.192
and starting fresh in California,

00:16:26.192 --> 00:16:28.620
it's so tempting and exhilarating.

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:29.453
- That is a theme here.

00:16:29.453 --> 00:16:31.980
It happened to Cortés, it's
happening to Fray Antonio.

00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:34.200
The guys on the ship are
like, "Dude, listen, Antonio

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:35.033
we get it,

00:16:35.033 --> 00:16:36.002
you want Spain to settle in California

00:16:36.002 --> 00:16:38.460
but that's not what this mission is about.

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:41.190
We're here to map the West
Coast of North America,

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:43.560
so just like chill out and do your job.

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:45.240
Sit around and be religious."

00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:48.120
And Fray Antonio, like,
continues to wax super nostalgic.

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:51.090
He's like "San Diego is a very fine bay,

00:16:51.090 --> 00:16:52.380
very fine anchorage,

00:16:52.380 --> 00:16:54.687
protected from all the
winds and undertow."

00:16:55.908 --> 00:16:58.020
Anyway, this expedition goes on

00:16:58.020 --> 00:16:59.580
to fulfill its original purpose,

00:16:59.580 --> 00:17:02.070
they mapped the West Coast of California

00:17:02.070 --> 00:17:04.080
and they create this decent looking map.

00:17:04.080 --> 00:17:06.214
But Fray Antonio walked
away from this mission

00:17:06.214 --> 00:17:08.160
with a very different view.

00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:10.860
He was convinced that
California was an island

00:17:10.860 --> 00:17:13.362
and that there was a
mythical northern passage

00:17:13.362 --> 00:17:15.900
that existed all the way back to Spain.

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:18.487
And he was so audacious to say, quote,

00:17:18.487 --> 00:17:21.360
"I believe it will be
necessary and important to use"

00:17:21.360 --> 00:17:23.407
what he's created, what he saw,

00:17:23.407 --> 00:17:26.700
"for revising and correcting
the existing maps of the Earth"

00:17:26.700 --> 00:17:30.150
since the existing maps
basically are very different

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:32.070
than what they are, in fact.

00:17:32.070 --> 00:17:34.350
Translation, Spain, you
need to change your maps

00:17:34.350 --> 00:17:36.600
to what I saw when I
was sitting there bored

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:37.560
looking over the boat,

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:39.060
because California is an island.

00:17:39.060 --> 00:17:41.700
I mean, the confidence in
this guy is astounding.

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:44.220
Fray Antonio, like, makes
a big ruckus about this.

00:17:44.220 --> 00:17:45.840
He tries to get Spain to change their maps

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:46.920
to make California an island.

00:17:46.920 --> 00:17:49.140
It got to the point where the
official map maker of Spain

00:17:49.140 --> 00:17:52.020
had to actually say, dude, you're wrong.

00:17:52.020 --> 00:17:55.740
He said, quote, "Since no
proof of the lake of gold

00:17:55.740 --> 00:17:56.970
or Strait of Anián",

00:17:56.970 --> 00:17:59.040
which is that northern strait, "exists,

00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:02.190
and since California has
not been circumnavigated,

00:18:02.190 --> 00:18:04.650
little credit should be
given to these concepts."

00:18:04.650 --> 00:18:06.780
Like dude, Fray Antonio, you're fake news,

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:08.520
you've been debunked, give it up.

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:10.260
But Fray Antonio does not give it up.

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:11.501
For the next two decades,

00:18:11.501 --> 00:18:15.780
he continues to pitch his
fake version of California.

00:18:15.780 --> 00:18:18.420
Sending loads of reports and maps

00:18:18.420 --> 00:18:19.860
of his version of California,

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:20.760
what he saw,

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:23.370
all with the hope that
Spain would catch on

00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:24.990
and come settle California.

00:18:24.990 --> 00:18:27.957
And he started to actually
gain some followers.

00:18:27.957 --> 00:18:29.895
He was pitching this, he
was like on a campaign

00:18:29.895 --> 00:18:32.700
to like spread this idea
that California is an island,

00:18:32.700 --> 00:18:34.080
I saw it with my own eyes.

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:35.370
And this is where it gets crazy

00:18:35.370 --> 00:18:38.700
because guess who enters
the story, but pirates.

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:42.300
Early 1600s is like the
golden age for pirates.

00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:44.284
Thousands of pirates were sailing the seas

00:18:44.284 --> 00:18:47.550
and because Spain was like
the best at stealing stuff

00:18:47.550 --> 00:18:48.570
and conquering places,

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:51.660
a Spanish ship was always
like jackpot for the pirates.

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:53.160
One such Spanish ship

00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:56.100
was sailing back from
the new world to Spain

00:18:56.100 --> 00:18:59.430
and onboard this ship was
one of Fray Antonio's letters

00:18:59.430 --> 00:19:01.110
with a copy of his fake map

00:19:01.110 --> 00:19:03.150
that showed California as an island.

00:19:03.150 --> 00:19:06.240
And the ship was taken
over by Dutch pirates.

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:08.850
They took the ship, they
steal all the stuff,

00:19:08.850 --> 00:19:12.390
and they stumble upon this
BS fake map from Fray Antonio

00:19:12.390 --> 00:19:15.330
like a deepfake of the
map of North America.

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:17.160
And the pirates are
like, whoa, look at this.

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:20.580
The Spanish have California as an island.

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:21.420
Little context here,

00:19:21.420 --> 00:19:23.520
maps back then were kind
of a national secret,

00:19:23.520 --> 00:19:25.380
they didn't share maps very much.

00:19:25.380 --> 00:19:26.790
So these Dutch guys are like holy shit,

00:19:26.790 --> 00:19:28.650
we just discovered a big Spanish secret

00:19:28.650 --> 00:19:30.480
that California is an island

00:19:30.480 --> 00:19:32.310
and that there's actually a strait

00:19:32.310 --> 00:19:34.260
that goes over the top of North America.

00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:36.390
And they've been keeping
it from us the whole time.

00:19:36.390 --> 00:19:39.720
So this map from Fray Antonio
gets back to the Netherlands

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:40.553
which, at the time,

00:19:40.553 --> 00:19:43.020
were rising as like really
good map makers and sailors.

00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:44.515
And boom.

00:19:44.515 --> 00:19:48.450
A few years later, the fake
news just got retweeted.

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:49.590
Here it is,

00:19:49.590 --> 00:19:52.710
this little map on the cover
page of some Dutch Atlas.

00:19:52.710 --> 00:19:53.929
You see, for the first time,

00:19:53.929 --> 00:19:57.030
California not connected to the mainland.

00:19:57.030 --> 00:19:58.860
But wait, Cortés already mapped this

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:01.710
and showed that this wasn't
an island like 100 years ago.

00:20:01.710 --> 00:20:03.210
No, what is happening?

00:20:03.210 --> 00:20:06.090
The answer is Fray Antonio and pirates.

00:20:06.090 --> 00:20:07.650
The myth was back.

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:09.660
Shortly after that, another map pops up.

00:20:09.660 --> 00:20:11.640
The Dutch are slowly adopting the idea

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:13.320
of California as an island

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:15.540
and, in true fake news form,

00:20:15.540 --> 00:20:18.330
everyone else was like, well
the Dutch they're legit,

00:20:18.330 --> 00:20:19.950
they said that California is an island,

00:20:19.950 --> 00:20:21.870
so I guess it is.

00:20:21.870 --> 00:20:23.430
Word eventually spread to London,

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:27.063
where a famous map maker made this map.

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:30.780
Wait a minute, I know this map.

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:32.910
This is the one that
originally got me interested

00:20:32.910 --> 00:20:34.260
in this whole entire topic.

00:20:34.260 --> 00:20:35.730
Whoa, hold on.

00:20:35.730 --> 00:20:38.670
And now I finally understand what it says

00:20:38.670 --> 00:20:39.817
in this lower left.

00:20:39.817 --> 00:20:42.480
"California, sometimes supposed to be

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:44.430
a part of the Western continent

00:20:44.430 --> 00:20:49.430
but since by a Spanish
chart taken by Hollanders,

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:55.163
it is found to be a goodly island."

00:20:55.163 --> 00:20:57.415
British cartographer, verified account,

00:20:57.415 --> 00:21:00.540
retweets Fray Antonio's delusion

00:21:00.540 --> 00:21:04.770
and suddenly this map with
California as an island

00:21:04.770 --> 00:21:08.133
is legitimized and starts
to spread like wildfire.

00:21:09.007 --> 00:21:10.745
"Seems legit" says a Dutch cartographer,

00:21:10.745 --> 00:21:12.120
boom, retweet.

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:15.150
Then to Germany, then to
France, then to Italy,

00:21:15.150 --> 00:21:16.230
then to Sweden

00:21:16.230 --> 00:21:18.720
and Turkey is now drawing
California as an island

00:21:18.720 --> 00:21:21.330
and Austria is drawing
California as an island.

00:21:21.330 --> 00:21:24.420
And now Japan is drawing
California as an island.

00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:26.580
Retweet, retweet, retweet.

00:21:26.580 --> 00:21:29.250
Oof, man, where are the mods on this one?

00:21:29.250 --> 00:21:31.080
Who's gonna fact check this?

00:21:31.080 --> 00:21:34.407
The entire globe now thinks
that California is an island.

00:21:34.407 --> 00:21:36.540
And so, yeah, this is how it happened.

00:21:36.540 --> 00:21:38.160
Lots of wishful thinking,

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:41.340
a few crazy coincidences with pirates.

00:21:41.340 --> 00:21:42.927
The whole world thinks
California's an island

00:21:42.927 --> 00:21:46.170
for 200 years, 200 years,

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:47.880
that's like several lifetimes.

00:21:47.880 --> 00:21:51.089
All because this one guy was
obsessed with a fake vision

00:21:51.089 --> 00:21:52.590
and he wouldn't let it go.

00:21:52.590 --> 00:21:53.423
Now, of course,

00:21:53.423 --> 00:21:55.650
eventually explorers would
go and fact check this

00:21:55.650 --> 00:21:56.483
and debunk it,

00:21:56.483 --> 00:21:57.916
and maps, slowly but surely,

00:21:57.916 --> 00:22:00.840
start to phase out
California as an island.

00:22:00.840 --> 00:22:03.388
But I wanna argue that this
wasn't just Fray Antonio

00:22:03.388 --> 00:22:04.980
and his wishful thinking,

00:22:04.980 --> 00:22:07.920
I kind of think that
California does this to people.

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:10.830
Starting with Cortés and
continuing to this day,

00:22:10.830 --> 00:22:14.640
California has continued to
have a mythical pull on people,

00:22:14.640 --> 00:22:16.583
at least it has on European people

00:22:16.583 --> 00:22:19.140
coming to California looking
for something magical,

00:22:19.140 --> 00:22:20.220
looking for gold,

00:22:20.220 --> 00:22:21.808
eventually actually finding gold.

00:22:21.808 --> 00:22:23.850
Like, which is kind of insane to me

00:22:23.850 --> 00:22:28.850
that the prophecy from a
1510 novel written in Spain

00:22:29.250 --> 00:22:31.830
kind of came true in 1849,

00:22:31.830 --> 00:22:34.560
when a bunch of people
found gold in California.

00:22:34.560 --> 00:22:35.580
As the local people

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:37.530
who had been in California
for thousands of years

00:22:37.530 --> 00:22:41.370
were exterminated and replaced
by industrious Europeans,

00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:44.730
California remained and
continues to be a place

00:22:44.730 --> 00:22:46.890
where people go to dream big.

00:22:46.890 --> 00:22:48.570
- Palo Alto's kind of this mythical place

00:22:48.570 --> 00:22:50.459
where, you know, all
the startups come from.

00:22:50.459 --> 00:22:51.507
- [Johnny] Like Cortés,

00:22:51.507 --> 00:22:54.120
who spent all of his money
trying to like figure out

00:22:54.120 --> 00:22:55.050
what California was

00:22:55.050 --> 00:22:57.813
or Fray Antonio, who spent
a good part of his life

00:22:57.813 --> 00:23:01.110
trying to convince people
that California was an island,

00:23:01.110 --> 00:23:02.010
something special

00:23:02.010 --> 00:23:03.720
and, ultimately, it worked.

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:05.760
People go to California to chase dreams

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:07.230
that usually aren't grounded

00:23:07.230 --> 00:23:08.580
in any sense of reality.

00:23:08.580 --> 00:23:10.500
- You've been on the cover of magazines,

00:23:10.500 --> 00:23:12.570
you could transform healthcare

00:23:12.570 --> 00:23:14.550
not only here in America,
but around the world.

00:23:14.550 --> 00:23:16.740
- They go and they push
and they push and they push

00:23:16.740 --> 00:23:18.300
on some vision of something new,

00:23:18.300 --> 00:23:21.780
even when those dreams rest
on spreading disinformation

00:23:21.780 --> 00:23:22.613
to the whole world.

00:23:22.613 --> 00:23:23.760
Was that too on the nose?

00:23:23.760 --> 00:23:25.200
Am I stretching the analogy too much

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:27.090
with disinformation and propaganda

00:23:27.090 --> 00:23:28.860
and Fray Antonio and Silicon Valley?

00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:30.450
Like, are you getting this?

00:23:30.450 --> 00:23:31.283
I'll stop.

00:23:31.283 --> 00:23:33.990
The point is that, to me, this
is the beauty and the peril

00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:35.820
of California as a place.

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:37.410
Yeah, it's attached to the continent,

00:23:37.410 --> 00:23:38.243
we all know that

00:23:38.243 --> 00:23:40.590
but California kind of is an island.

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:44.158
Ecologically, it has thousands
of totally unique species

00:23:44.158 --> 00:23:45.450
of flora and fauna

00:23:45.450 --> 00:23:46.740
that don't exist anywhere else.

00:23:46.740 --> 00:23:50.310
Economically, it's the fifth
biggest economy on Earth.

00:23:50.310 --> 00:23:53.460
And culturally, I mean,
California is its own culture.

00:23:53.460 --> 00:23:55.290
And the result is that, today,

00:23:55.290 --> 00:23:57.570
people still do what those guys were doing

00:23:57.570 --> 00:23:59.610
in the 15/1600s,

00:23:59.610 --> 00:24:02.580
coming west to California to chase dreams,

00:24:02.580 --> 00:24:04.980
to chase gold, to chase something new.

00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:07.890
In that sense, California is an island,

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:09.960
Fray Antonio was right the whole time.

00:24:09.960 --> 00:24:11.283
Okay, see ya.

00:24:13.485 --> 00:24:16.902
(uplifting techno music)

